--TEST--
`Core\Request::bytes` answers `tainted bytes`, so a plain `bytes` binding refuses it exactly as one refuses a `bodyStream` chunk — spec § 15, `rule:security/tainted-qualifier`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The qualifier is what a second body reader could quietly drop: `body()`
// answers `tainted string` over these same octets, so a `bytes()` that handed
// back a plain value would be a launderer reachable by spelling the binary
// reading instead of the textual one. No runtime assertion can show it -- a
// plain value is assignable into a `tainted` target, so a case that only bound
// one would pass over a member that had already dropped the mark. The refusal
// is the pin, and it is the binding that has to fail: `bytes` narrows through
// no qualifier bit.
bytes $raw = Core\Request::bytes();
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `bytes`, found `tainted bytes`
   --> case.nvs:10:14
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
